Chapter 1
Cost behaviour, contribution margin and the breakeven point
سلوك التكاليف وهامش المساهمة ونقطة التعادل
Orontes sold $42,000,000 of food in 2025 and earned $4,176,000 of operating income. Those two figures, and the relationship between them, are what this book is about. Book 1 read them as history. This book asks what happens if the company sells a tenth more, or charges fifty cents less, or stops making one of its products — and none of those questions can be answered from an income statement, because an income statement sorts costs by what they were spent on rather than by how they behave.
Sorting costs by behaviour is the first act of decision analysis, and everything in Section C follows from it. A cost that rises with volume and a cost that does not are different kinds of thing, and treating them alike is the single most expensive error in management accounting.
Figure F701-01 is your map.
Figure F701-01. Chapter 1 roadmap: two kinds of cost, one margin, and the point where profit begins.
