WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Book 1 computed Orontes's degree of operating leverage as 4.02, which it built from a contribution margin of $16,800,000 and an operating income of $4,176,000. Those two figures imply the whole of this chapter: variable costs of $25,200,000 on sales of $42,000,000, and fixed costs of $12,624,000. This chapter uses them in every section.
1.1  Two kinds of cost
A variable cost changes in total in proportion to the level of activity and stays constant per unit. Sesame, packaging, the electricity that runs the roaster, the piece rate paid to a packer: make twice as many jars and the total doubles, while each jar still carries the same amount.
A fixed cost stays constant in total over a range of activity and therefore falls per unit as volume rises. The factory lease, the depreciation on the roasting line, the plant manager's salary, the audit fee. Making twice as many jars does not make the lease larger, and it halves the lease carried by each jar.
Those two sentences contain the trap that costs most marks in Section C. In total, a variable cost moves and a fixed cost does not. Per unit, a variable cost does not move and a fixed cost does. Everything else in the chapter follows from keeping the two columns straight.
A mixed or semi-variable cost has both elements: a telephone contract with a standing charge and a per-call rate, a maintenance agreement with a retainer and an hourly rate, a factory's power bill with a capacity charge and a consumption charge. For decision purposes a mixed cost is split into its two parts, and Part 1 taught the methods — the high-low method and regression.
A step cost is fixed over a range and then jumps: one supervisor for every twenty packers, one delivery van for every so many routes. Over a wide enough range every fixed cost is a step cost, which is why the next section matters.
Figure F701-02 sets the behaviour out.
Figure F701-02. How each kind of cost behaves
@table
Cost | In total, as volume rises | Per unit, as volume rises | Orontes examples
Variable | Rises proportionately | Constant | Sesame, jars, labels, piece rates
Fixed | Constant | Falls | Factory lease, depreciation, salaries
Mixed | Rises, from a base | Falls towards the variable rate | Power, maintenance, telephone
Step | Constant, then jumps | Falls, then jumps | Supervisors, vans, shift premiums
@end
EXAM TRAP   In total or per unit
"Fixed costs per unit fall as volume rises" is true and is not a contradiction of "fixed costs do not change". Read which column a question is asking about.
A cost is not fixed because it is unavoidable, and not variable because it is large. The test is whether it moves with output.
Direct and indirect is a different classification entirely. A factory supervisor's salary is indirect and fixed; sesame is direct and variable; a royalty of fifty cents a jar is direct and variable; factory rent is indirect and fixed. The two classifications cut across each other.
