1.2  Why the classification depends on the time frame
No cost is fixed for ever and almost none is fixed instantly. The classification describes a period and a range, and the exam tests whether a candidate knows it.
Over a very short period almost everything is fixed. Tomorrow's output cannot change the lease, the salaries, the insurance, or the sesame already bought and in the silo. The only genuinely variable costs of tomorrow are the ones not yet committed.
Over a long enough period almost everything is variable. Given three years, Orontes can leave the factory, sell the roasting line, change the size of its workforce and renegotiate every contract. There are no fixed costs in a long enough run, which is why the distinction is never absolute.
In between lies the period the decision concerns, and that is the period the classification must describe. A question about accepting an order next month treats the plant as fixed; a question about opening a second plant does not.
The relevant range carries the same idea for volume rather than time. Fixed costs are fixed and variable costs are proportionate only within the range of activity the company is actually operating in. Below it, a company closes departments; above it, it hires supervisors, adds a shift, leases a second warehouse, and pays overtime rates that are not the normal variable rate. A cost-volume-profit analysis is valid inside the relevant range and nowhere else, and saying so is worth a mark.
Figure F701-03 shows the same cost at three horizons.
Figure F701-03. One cost, three time frames
@table
Cost | Next week | Next year | Over three years
Factory lease | Fixed | Fixed | Variable; the lease can be ended
Supervisors' salaries | Fixed | Step; a shift can be added or cut | Variable
Sesame | Variable | Variable | Variable
Depreciation on the roaster | Fixed | Fixed | Variable; the asset can be sold
@end
