1.3  Contribution margin
Contribution margin is what is left of a selling price after the variable costs of making and selling that unit. It is called contribution because it is what each unit contributes, first to covering the fixed costs and then, once those are covered, to operating income.
Orontes's main line is a one-kilogram jar of tahini selling at $12.00, with variable costs of $7.20 a jar. The contribution margin is 12.00 − 7.20 = 4.80 a jar, and the contribution margin ratio is 4.80 ÷ 12.00 = 40.0 per cent.
Those figures reconcile to the company as a whole. Orontes sold 42,000,000 ÷ 12.00 = 3,500,000 jars, which at $4.80 each gives 3,500,000 × 4.80 = 16,800,000 of total contribution margin — the figure Book 1 used to compute a degree of operating leverage of 4.02. Deducting fixed costs of $12,624,000 leaves 16,800,000 − 12,624,000 = 4,176,000, which is the operating income on the face of the income statement.
That reconciliation is worth pausing on. The contribution income statement and the ordinary one report the same operating income and sort the costs differently, and only one of them can answer a question about volume.
Figure F701-04 sets the two side by side.
Figure F701-04. Two income statements, one result
@table
By function, as reported | | By behaviour, for decisions |
Net sales | 42,000,000 | Net sales | 42,000,000
Cost of goods sold | (27,300,000) | Variable costs | (25,200,000)
Gross profit | 14,700,000 | Contribution margin | 16,800,000
Operating expenses | (10,524,000) | Fixed costs | (12,624,000)
Operating income | 4,176,000 | Operating income | 4,176,000
@end
Note what moved. Cost of goods sold of $27,300,000 is not the variable cost: it contains the fixed factory costs absorbed into the product, and it excludes the variable part of selling and distribution. Gross profit and contribution margin are different measures and a candidate who uses one for the other will get every breakeven question wrong.
