1.5  What the model assumes
Cost-volume-profit analysis is a model, and a candidate who can state its assumptions can also state when it should not be relied on. Six assumptions, each of which fails somewhere.
Costs can be split cleanly into fixed and variable. Mixed costs must be separated, and the separation is an estimate.
Behaviour is linear within the relevant range. Revenue rises in a straight line, which assumes the selling price does not change with volume — and Chapter 11 shows that it usually does.
The sales mix is constant. With several products, a change in which ones sell changes the average contribution, which is the whole of Chapter 3.
Volume is the only cost driver. Batch sizes, order sizes, product complexity and customer behaviour all drive costs too, which is the argument for activity-based costing.
Inventory does not change. Production equals sales, so no fixed cost is carried forward in stock. Where inventory moves, absorption costing and variable costing report different incomes, as Part 1 showed.
Everything else is held constant: prices, costs, efficiency, technology.
None of this makes the model useless. It makes it a first approximation that is right about direction and approximate about magnitude, and the professional habit is to state the assumption that matters most to the decision in hand. For Orontes, that assumption is the constant selling price, because the company's gross margin has fallen three years running — which means its revenue line is not as straight as the model draws it.
