Case-style practice: the board asks what a bad year looks like
Orontes's board asks the finance director what a 10 per cent fall in volume would do, and what the company would have to sell to hold its operating income at $4,176,000 if the selling price had to be cut by 50 cents a jar. The jar sells at $12.00 with variable costs of $7.20, fixed costs are $12,624,000, and the company sold 3,500,000 jars.
Required. Answer both questions with figures, and say which of the two scenarios is the more dangerous and why. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC1-1  Answer A. Total rises proportionately; the unit amount does not move.
B is wrong. That describes a fixed cost.
C is wrong. Variable costs rise with volume.
D is wrong. The per-unit figure is constant.
SC1-2  Answer A. The total is unchanged and is spread over more units.
B is wrong. The total does not rise within the relevant range.
C is wrong. The per-unit figure falls.
D is wrong. The total is constant, not falling.
SC1-3  Answer A. The cost is fixed over a range and then jumps.
B is wrong. It does not rise with each unit.
C is wrong. There is no per-unit usage rate.
D is wrong. Supervision is a production cost.
SC1-4  Answer A. Given enough time, every contract can be changed.
B is wrong. The direction is the opposite.
C is wrong. Costs behave; the classification changes.
D is wrong. The relevant range is about volume, not time.
SC1-5  Answer A. It is the range over which the assumptions hold.
B is wrong. Prices are a separate matter.
C is wrong. The time frame is the separate idea of section 1.2.
D is wrong. The margin of safety is Chapter 4.
SC1-6  Answer A. Within a month the lease cannot be changed.
B is wrong. Lease payments do not move with output.
C is wrong. Indirect does not mean irrelevant.
D is wrong. A single lease does not step within the month.
SC1-7  Answer A. Price less variable cost per unit.
B is wrong. Total cost includes fixed costs.
C is wrong. Gross profit deducts absorbed fixed factory costs.
D is wrong. Operating income is after all fixed costs.
SC1-8  Answer A. 4.80 ÷ 12.00 = 40.0 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the variable cost ratio.
C is wrong. That is the margin in dollars, not a ratio.
D is wrong. 35.0 per cent is the gross margin.
SC1-9  Answer A. The two measures contain different costs.
B is wrong. Neither is after tax.
C is wrong. Contribution margin is before fixed costs.
D is wrong. They differ for Orontes by over two million dollars.
SC1-10  Answer A. Fixed costs over unit contribution.
B is wrong. The price has not covered its own variable cost.
C is wrong. Total costs include the variable costs already deducted.
D is wrong. This inverts the relationship.
SC1-11  Answer A. 12,624,000 ÷ 4.80 = 2,630,000 jars.
B is wrong. That is actual volume.
C is wrong. This divides by 12.00.
D is wrong. That is the breakeven in dollars.
SC1-12  Answer A. The target is added to the fixed costs.
B is wrong. This divides one by the other.
C is wrong. This omits the fixed costs.
D is wrong. The breakeven earns nothing.
SC1-13  Answer A. Constant prices, not changing ones, are assumed.
B is wrong. Linearity is assumed.
C is wrong. A constant mix is assumed.
D is wrong. Volume as the only driver is assumed.
SC1-14  Answer A. A falling margin means the price line is not straight.
B is wrong. Inventory is not the issue here.
C is wrong. Fixed costs are not what has moved.
D is wrong. Tax affects net income, not the contribution structure.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Contribution margin | 2,637,360: 21,978,000 − 19,340,640 = 2,637,360.
Contribution margin ratio | 12.0 per cent: 2,637,360 ÷ 21,978,000 = 12.0 per cent.
Fixed costs | 2,079,360: 2,637,360 − 558,000 = 2,079,360.
Breakeven in dollars | 17,328,000: 2,079,360 ÷ 0.12 = 17,328,000.
Sales for a 1,000,000 operating income | 25,661,333: 2,079,360 + 1,000,000 = 3,079,360, and 3,079,360 ÷ 0.12 = 25,661,333.
What it tells you | Barada breaks even at about 79 per cent of its current sales, against Orontes's 75 per cent, and its 12 per cent contribution ratio means every extra dollar of sales brings only twelve cents towards fixed costs. To earn a million it would have to grow sales by about 17 per cent in a year — which, as Book 2 showed, it cannot fund.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P1-01  Answer A. Constant in total, falling per unit.
B is wrong. That is a variable cost.
C is wrong. The total does not fall.
D is wrong. The per-unit figure falls.
P1-02  Answer A. Sesame rises with every jar.
B is wrong. The lease does not move with output.
C is wrong. A salary is fixed within the period.
D is wrong. Depreciation is fixed.
P1-03  Answer A. Over three years the lease can be ended.
B is wrong. Nothing is fixed for ever.
C is wrong. A single lease is not a step cost over that horizon.
D is wrong. It is highly relevant to a three-year decision.
P1-04  Answer A. 12.00 − 7.20 = 4.80.
B is wrong. That is the variable cost.
C is wrong. That is the price.
D is wrong. That is the ratio expressed as a decimal.
P1-05  Answer A. 4.80 ÷ 12.00 = 40.0 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the variable cost ratio.
C is wrong. 35.0 per cent is the gross margin.
D is wrong. This divides 4.80 by 7.20.
P1-06  Answer A. 12,624,000 ÷ 4.80 = 2,630,000 units.
B is wrong. The answer is a quantity, not an amount.
