Case-style set: the board asks what a bad year looks like
A 10 per cent fall in volume. Ten per cent of 3,500,000 jars is 350,000 jars, each carrying a contribution of $4.80, so operating income falls by 350,000 × 4.80 = 1,680,000 — from $4,176,000 to $2,496,000, a fall of about 40 per cent. The arithmetic is the operating leverage of 4.02 that Book 1 measured: a ten per cent fall in sales takes four times that proportion off the profit, because the fixed costs of $12,624,000 do not move.
A 50-cent price cut. The contribution margin becomes 12.00 − 0.50 = 11.50 less variable costs of 7.20, which is 11.50 − 7.20 = 4.30 a jar. To hold operating income at $4,176,000 the company needs 12,624,000 + 4,176,000 = 16,800,000 of contribution, which at $4.30 a jar is 16,800,000 ÷ 4.30 = 3,906,977 jars — about 407,000 more jars, or 11.6 per cent more volume, to stand still.
Which is more dangerous. The price cut, and by a wide margin. The volume fall is a bad year and recovers when volume recovers; the price cut is permanent in the way Book 1 described, and it demands an 11.6 per cent volume gain every year thereafter merely to hold the same profit. Worse, the two scenarios are not independent: a price cut made to defend volume in a weak market is exactly the combination that produces both at once, and on these figures a 50-cent cut that failed to add volume would take operating income to 3,500,000 × 4.30 = 15,050,000 of contribution, which is 15,050,000 − 12,624,000 = 2,426,000 — slightly worse than losing a tenth of the volume outright.
